    "description": "Parliamentary committees organize specialist scrutiny within Iran’s Islamic Consultative Assembly, commonly called the Majles. Their documented responsibilities connect legislative review with examination of government performance, although a committee report does not by itself establish that its recommendations were implemented.",
    "tags": ["Parliament", "Committees", "Legislative scrutiny", "Public administration"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:24:14Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:24:14Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Parliamentary Committees in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "How Iran’s parliamentary committees review legislation, examine implementation, and contribute to budget and development-plan scrutiny."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "specialist-scrutiny",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Where specialist scrutiny happens",
        "content": "The Majles’s internal rules distinguish specialized committees from committees with particular procedural responsibilities. This distinction matters when reading parliamentary news: the word “committee” does not identify a single kind of body with identical powers. Article 38 of the inspected Persian compilation establishes these separate categories. Its text also records a later amendment concerning committees that cannot reach their normal membership threshold, illustrating why historical descriptions of committee composition require checking against subsequent changes. [s1]\n\nFor international readers, the useful starting point is a committee’s remit and the document it has produced. A report on a proposed law, an examination of administrative performance, and a recommendation concerning public expenditure represent different parliamentary actions. Article 45 assigns specialized committees responsibilities across these activities rather than restricting them to drafting legislation. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "legislation-and-implementation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Legislation, budgets, and implementation",
        "content": "Under Article 45, specialized committees examine referred legislative proposals and report to the chamber. They also consider investigation requests and parliamentary questions. Their budgetary work includes reviewing relevant portions of development plans and annual budgets and reporting to the consolidation committee, which brings contributions together. The same article provides for examining implementation reports and preparing assessments of executive bodies and the operation of legislation. [s2]\n\nThese provisions describe formal responsibilities. They do not establish how consistently committees obtain information, whether recommendations change administrative behavior, or how much influence individual committees exercise. Assessing those questions requires following identifiable reports through subsequent parliamentary and executive action. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "expert-input",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How research can enter committee work",
        "content": "Article 142 describes a route for referring a proposal to an expert working group at the Parliamentary Research Center when the lead committee considers this necessary and approves the referral. The group submits its analysis to that committee. This creates a documented connection between technical assessment and parliamentary deliberation; it does not make researchers the decision-making committee. [s3]\n\nA useful reading sequence is therefore to identify the proposal, locate its expert assessment where available, and compare that assessment with the committee’s eventual report. Differences between these documents can reveal which recommendations advanced and which remained advisory. This is a method for examining the documentary record, rather than a claim that every proposal follows an equally transparent path. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
