    "description": "The Parliamentary Research Center is a research institution attached to Iran’s parliament, with a statutory mandate to support legislative analysis and parliamentary information needs. Its expert assessments contribute to deliberation, while claims about their practical influence require evidence beyond the institution’s own descriptions.",
    "tags": ["Parliamentary research", "Legislation", "Policy analysis", "Majles"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:24:14Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:24:14Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Parliamentary Research Center | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "The statutory role of Iran’s Parliamentary Research Center, its expert working groups, and the distinction between research and legislative authority."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "statutory-purpose",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A research institution serving parliament",
        "content": "The Parliamentary Research Center’s duties law describes an institution attached to the Majles, Iran’s parliament. Its purpose is to strengthen expert examination of legislation and provide scientific and research support appropriate to parliamentary responsibilities. The statutory role extends beyond commenting on draft wording: it includes studying implementation problems, supplying information to representatives and committees, and gathering views from researchers and other interested bodies. [s1]\n\nThe law also identifies a board of trustees, a head of the Center, and a research council. It assigns the trustees responsibilities concerning policy, priorities, oversight, and appointment of the head. These provisions establish a parliamentary institutional relationship; they should not be read as evidence that the Center is an independent evaluator of parliament itself. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "working-groups",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From expertise to committee deliberation",
        "content": "Article 142 of the inspected parliamentary rules provides a concrete mechanism for expert work. With the lead committee’s approval, a legislative proposal can be referred to a Research Center working group. Its membership can draw on the Center’s specialists and outside expertise, including academic institutions, executive bodies, the private sector, and parliamentary specialists. [s2]\n\nThe working group examines the proposal and reports to the lead committee. Its analysis may consider legislative history and, where necessary, comparative experience. Importantly, the procedure allows a reasoned conclusion that new legislation is unnecessary. Research support therefore need not mean endorsing the introduction of another law. The group’s representative is expected to explain and defend its report in committee. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "institutional-claims",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading institutional claims critically",
        "content": "In an address published with the Asian Parliamentary Assembly’s 2022 research forum, the Center’s head described expert consultation, meetings with executive bodies, and research services for members of parliament. The address presented these activities as contributing to stronger lawmaking and oversight. It is useful evidence of the institution’s stated approach, but it is an institutional account rather than an independent evaluation of results. [s3]\n\nFor a reader evaluating a particular report, the central questions are its evidence, methodology, date, and relationship to the proposal under discussion. A persuasive assessment of influence would then compare its recommendations with committee revisions, the enacted text, and implementation evidence. The sources inspected here establish a research mandate and a procedural route into deliberation; they do not establish a measurable rate of policy success. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
